<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">South Yemen</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1967">1967</a>–<a href="/years/1990">1990</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as People's Democratic Republic of Yemen</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">South <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yemen">Yemen</a>, from 1970 the People's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/democratic-republic-of-yemen">Democratic Republic of Yemen</a>, was the state formed in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a> when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> withdrew from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aden">Aden</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-arabia">South Arabian</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorates</a> in November 1967. Ruled by the Marxist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yemeni-socialist-party">Yemeni Socialist Party</a>, it was the only communist state in the Arab world and was closely allied with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a>. Weakened by the civil war of 1986, it united with North Yemen in 1990.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/south-yemen.jpg" alt="South Yemen" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: National Front - الجبهة القومية · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_South_Yemen.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>South <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yemen">Yemen</a>'s origins go back to 1874 and the creation of the British Colony of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aden">Aden</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aden-protectorate">Aden Protectorate</a>. Before 1937 Aden had been governed as part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-india">British India</a>. In 1959 six <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorate</a> states signed an accord to form the Federation of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">Emirates</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-arabia">South Arabia</a>, and on 18 January 1963 Aden Colony was merged with it as the Federation of South Arabia.</p><p>The first uprising against British rule came in Radfan on 14 October 1963, when about 7,000 armed tribesmen joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-liberation-front-south-yemen">National Liberation Front</a>, which used hit-and-run tactics. In November 1967 the Federal Army came out in support of the NLF and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> negotiated a hasty handover.</p><p>The last British troops left eleven hours before the People's Republic of Southern Yemen was born at midnight on 29–30 November 1967, ending 128 years of colonial rule, and it joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a> on 14 December 1967.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-front-czechoslovakia">National Front</a>, as the NLF was now called, had around 26,000 members and no experience in government, and it split into right and left wings. On 22 June 1969 the Marxist–Leninist faction under Abdel Fattah Ismail and Salim Rubai Ali overthrew President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qahtan-al-shaabi">Qahtan al-Shaabi</a> in a bloodless coup called the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/corrective-move">Corrective Move</a>. A constitution drafted with Egyptian and East German advisers was promulgated on 30 November 1970, and the Front was replaced by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yemeni-socialist-party">Yemeni Socialist Party</a>, formalised in 1978.</p><p>The state received aid from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-germany">East Germany</a>, which stationed several hundred Stasi officers there, though by the mid-1980s the Soviet Union under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mikhail-gorbachev">Mikhail Gorbachev</a> had largely distanced itself.</p><p>On 13 January 1986 fighting broke out in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aden">Aden</a> between supporters of Ali Nasir and of the returned Ismail. It lasted more than a month, with thousands of casualties, Ali Nasir's ouster and Ismail's presumed death, and some 60,000 people fled to North Yemen. The People's Democratic Republic of Yemen united with the Yemen Arab Republic on 22 May 1990.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>South <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yemen">Yemen</a> was a centralised Marxist–Leninist state in which legislative authority rested with the Supreme People's Council and the official ideology was scientific socialism. Its economy moved from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aden">Aden</a>'s colonial port system to central planning, land reform and state-backed cooperatives, and major petroleum deposits were found in Shabwah Governorate in late 1986. The state set up a welfare system with health care and education, and the Family Law of 1974 banned child marriage and polygamy.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “South Yemen” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Republic of Yemen was formed on 22 May 1990 by the union of the Yemen Arab Republic in the north, with its capital at Sanaa, and the <b>People's Democratic Republic of Yemen</b>, or South Yemen, with its capital at Aden.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-yemen">Republic of Yemen · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote><b>South Yemen</b> was the only openly communist state in the Arab world.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/marxism-leninism">Marxism–Leninism · Spread &amp; Variants</a></li><li><blockquote>The rare lasting unions are the forcible unification of much of the Arabian Peninsula by the Saudi rulers of Najd in the 1920s, the United Arab Emirates and the unification of North and <b>South Yemen</b>.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/pan-arabism">Pan-Arabism · In Power</a></li></ul></section>
